Artefact 2: Data-profiling specification (v1.0)

Purpose: establish, for each candidate domain, whether G0 can pass and which design assumptions hold. Output is aggregate only.

Handling rules:

Runs in the secure layer. No row-level text leaves it.
Counts below 10 are suppressed.
Owner fields are excluded entirely.
Each check reports the denominator alongside the count.
A. Population and eligibility
Check	Measure
A1	Closed issues per domain by closure year and current rating
A2	Original versus current rating, and share downgraded before closure
A3	Eligible count under each entry route R1 to R7 (using placeholder thresholds), plus overlap between routes
A4	Closed fewer than 90 days before extract
A5	Umbrella candidates: distribution of linked action count, with the top 1% listed by count only
A6	Issues with zero linked actions (no-action cohort size)
A7	Existence and reliability of any structured independent-review field (population rate and value distribution)
B. Narrative usability
Check	Measure
B1	Population rate and length distribution for issue description, root cause, control-gap detail, issue closure summary
B2	Action description and action closure summary population rate and length distribution
B3	Boilerplate rate: share of text appearing verbatim or near-verbatim in more than 20 records
B4	Control-gap detail population when the indicator is set (tests indicator reliability)
B5	Rater-confirmed usable narrative: two analysts read 50 random eligible cases per domain and mark each narrative component usable or not. Length is only a screening proxy. The G0 figure comes from the rater result.
C. Action sets
Check	Measure
C1	Actions per eligible issue (distribution), and share of issues with 1, 2 to 3, 4 or more
C2	Action status distribution, and cancelled share
C3	Cancellation reason populated rate
C4	Completion date populated for completed actions, and conflicting statuses
C5	Whether status history and revised due dates exist (decides whether cancellations and extensions can be reconstructed)
C6	Whether any “required for closure” or dependency field exists (decides LLAC versus an explicit anchor)
C7	Share of actions that are generic types (training, reminder, procedure update)
D. Dates
Check	Measure
D1	Population and format consistency for each date in the temporal model
D2	Order violations: completion before action creation, closure before identification, completion in the future
D3	Issue closed before LLAC, and actions completed after closure
D4	Share with all linked actions cancelled
D5	Share of eligible cases with at least 6 months of post-LLAC observation before the extract
E. Links
Check	Measure
E1	Eligible issues with at least one recorded incident link, and with an action link
E2	Link plausibility signals: incident dated before issue identification, link counts per issue, orphaned references
E3	Link table duplicates and one-to-many patterns
F. Incidents
Check	Measure
F1	Description and cause population rate and length distribution
F2	Occurrence, identification and logging date population and lag distribution
F3	Duplicate identifier and bulk indicator population and prevalence
F4	Impact and financial field population
F5	Process and application reference coverage, and share that resolve to the process and application tables
F6	Pool size: for each eligible issue, the in-scope incident count by process hierarchy and application. Report the share of issues with fewer than 20 or more than 2,000 candidates.
F7	History depth: share of eligible issues with at least 12 months of incident history before identification
F8	Source continuity: monthly incident counts per process, with gaps and step changes flagged for system or definition changes
G. Scope references
Check	Measure
G1	Process and application references populated on issues and on actions
G2	Hierarchy level at which references sit, and share that do not resolve
G3	Share of issues whose scope is a single process (scope comparison not applicable)
H. Taxonomy and eras
Check	Measure
H1	Root cause hierarchy value counts by year, with additions, retirements and relabelling
H2	Change log of field definitions, closure requirements and source systems (a document request to data owners, not a query)
H3	Proposed era boundaries, derived from H1 and H2 and agreed before any sampling
I. Snapshots and duplicates
Check	Measure
I1	Multiple versions or snapshots per issue, action or incident
I2	Near-duplicate issues (same text, different identifiers)
J. Reporting indicators
Check	Measure
J1	Per process-quarter: median logging lag, share logged as No Impact or Low, logging volume. Feasibility only, not interpretation.
K. Privacy
Check	Measure
K1	Personal names in free text: rate on a rater-reviewed sample of 100 records across issue, action and incident text
K2	Customer identifiers, account numbers or other sensitive content in incident text, same sample
K3	Share of entity mentions (applications, products, vendors, channels) that a candidate scrubber would wrongly remove, tested on the same sample
G0 mapping
G0 condition	Source checks
Usable issue and action narrative in at least 80%	B5
Valid closure dates in at least 90%	D1, D2
Issue-action link or explicit no-action record in at least 80%	E1, A6
Usable incident narrative in at least 80%	F1 and a rater check on 50 incidents
At least 6 months post-window for at least 50%	D5

Checks with no threshold (F6, F7, F8, C5, C6, A7) decide design choices, not pass or fail. Each one gets an “implication” line in the output.

Output

One page per domain, a G0 table with pass, fail or borderline per condition, a list of design implications (for example “no required-action flag, so LLAC applies”), and a data-quality defect list for the data owners. Defects found here are a deliverable in themselves.
